WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.197
Canada versus the world.

00:00:02.147 --> 00:00:03.818
How we compare.

00:00:03.818 --> 00:00:17.443
This page ranks Canada against OECD peer nations on the metrics that define institutional performance: healthcare wait times, wireless prices, housing affordability, defence spending, MAID rates, drug costs, and democratic accountability.

00:00:17.443 --> 00:00:23.318
The data comes from the OECD, the World Bank, NATO, and international comparative studies.

00:00:23.318 --> 00:00:26.409
Canada is not the worst in every category.

00:00:26.409 --> 00:00:32.818
But the pattern is consistent: on nearly every metric that matters to citizens, Canada underperforms its peers.

00:00:32.818 --> 00:00:36.113
The highest wireless prices in the developed world.

00:00:36.113 --> 00:00:38.931
Among the longest healthcare wait times.

00:00:38.931 --> 00:00:41.488
The lowest NATO defence spending.

00:00:41.488 --> 00:00:43.909
The highest MAID rates globally.

00:00:43.909 --> 00:00:50.829
This is the international context for the institutional capture documented across two hundred and twenty pages on this site.

00:00:50.829 --> 00:00:53.579
Other countries face similar pressures.

00:00:53.579 --> 00:00:56.931
Few have produced outcomes this consistently poor.

00:00:56.931 --> 00:00:58.954
Healthcare wait times.

00:00:58.954 --> 00:01:03.965
Canada ranks near the bottom among OECD countries for specialist wait times.

00:01:03.965 --> 00:01:05.943
Wireless prices.

00:01:05.943 --> 00:01:10.579
Canadians pay among the highest prices in the developed world for mobile service.

00:01:10.579 --> 00:01:12.545
Housing affordability.

00:01:12.545 --> 00:01:17.386
Canada's house price to income ratio exceeds most OECD peers.

00:01:17.386 --> 00:01:19.477
NATO defence spending.

00:01:19.477 --> 00:01:25.034
Canada spends one point two nine percent of GDP on defence against a two percent target.

00:01:25.034 --> 00:01:26.670
MAID rates.

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:32.568
Canada has the highest rate of medically assisted deaths in the world among countries that have legalized the practice.

00:01:32.568 --> 00:01:34.329
Drug prices.

00:01:34.329 --> 00:01:40.249
Canadian drug prices rank among the highest in the OECD despite the PMPRB's mandate.

00:01:40.249 --> 00:01:42.738
The pattern across every metric.

00:01:42.738 --> 00:01:46.022
Healthcare: worst wait times among peers.

00:01:46.022 --> 00:01:48.602
Telecoms: highest prices.

00:01:48.602 --> 00:01:51.568
Housing: among worst affordability.

00:01:51.568 --> 00:01:54.306
Defence: lowest NATO spending.

00:01:54.306 --> 00:01:56.852
MAID: highest rates globally.

00:01:56.852 --> 00:02:00.431
Drug prices: above OECD average.

00:02:00.431 --> 00:02:04.715
Each metric maps to a documented institutional failure on this site.

00:02:04.715 --> 00:02:07.863
Wait times map to healthcare privatization.

00:02:07.863 --> 00:02:11.215
Wireless prices map to regulatory capture.

00:02:11.215 --> 00:02:13.840
Housing maps to financialization.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:16.738
Defence maps to military degradation.

00:02:16.738 --> 00:02:20.068
MAID rates map to the immigration-MAID pipeline.

00:02:20.068 --> 00:02:23.454
Drug prices map to PMPRB capture.

00:02:23.454 --> 00:02:26.863
Other countries face similar institutional pressures.

00:02:26.863 --> 00:02:31.261
Few produce outcomes this consistently poor across this many domains.

00:02:31.261 --> 00:02:36.636
That consistency is the signature of systemic capture, not individual policy failure.
